<div class="evolution-framework-container">

  <div class="framework-header">
    <div class="header-icon" aria-hidden="true">🌍</div>
    <h2>PALEOANTHROPOLOGY SYNTHESIS</h2>
    <p>Mapping the Complex Branches of the Human Lineage</p>
  </div>

  <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #cbd5e1; margin-top: 2rem;">
    Understanding early human evolution requires evaluating a complex matrix of fossil morphology, paleoclimatic tracking, and genetic divergence timelines. This compilation provides a rigorous critical review of the landmark 2015 synthesis, filtering the competing models of early hominin divergence through a modern interdisciplinary lens.
  </p>

  <div class="framework-grid">
    
    <div class="theory-column" style="width: 100%;">
      <h3 class="column-title theory-title">🧬 Core Theoretical Pillars</h3>
      
      <details class="node-toggle theory-node" open="">
        <summary>I. Hominin Phylogeny &amp; Fossil Transitions</summary>
        <div class="node-content">
          <p>Tracing structural adaptations across the transition from late Miocene apes to early *Homo*. This panel evaluates macro-morphological shifts, cranial capacity scaling parameters, and locomotor changes observed across landmark fossil horizons.</p>
          <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #94a3b8; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
            <li><strong>Bipedalism Origins:</strong> Tracking skeletal indicators such as the positioning of the foramen magnum and pelvic basin remodeling.</li>
            <li><strong>Encephalization Trajectories:</strong> Assessing the energetic trade-offs associated with increasing brain volume relative to overall metabolic constraints.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="node-toggle theory-node">
        <summary>II. Environmental Drivers of Adaptation</summary>
        <div class="node-content">
          <p>Evaluating macro-evolutionary models against local paleoclimatic records. This area looks closely at environmental shifts that altered selective pressures and accelerated behavioral adjustments.</p>
          <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #94a3b8; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
            <li><strong>The Savannah Hypothesis:</strong> Analyzing how the open grasslands of the East African Rift System impacted the evolution of upright locomotion.</li>
            <li><strong>Variability Selection Paradigm:</strong> Evaluating how fluctuating climate cycles favored flexible, adaptive behavioral strategies over hyper-specialized physical traits.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="node-toggle theory-node">
        <summary>III. Lithic Tech-Nodes &amp; Behavioral Archeology</summary>
        <div class="node-content">
          <p>Analyzing the archaeological evidence for early cognitive leaps. The introduction of standardized tool preparation represents a monumental shift in resource processing and abstract planning capabilities.</p>
          <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; color: #94a3b8; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
            <li><strong>Oldowan Core Industries:</strong> Early stone tools indicating basic percussion fracturing techniques.</li>
            <li><strong>Acheulean Bifacial Advancements:</strong> Symmetrical handaxes that showcase mental templates and deliberate design planning.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

  </div>

